---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import register from "../data/register.json";

const rows = register.filter((r) => (r.surname || "").toUpperCase() === "ANNECCHINO");
const kinds = {};
for (const r of rows) kinds[r.kind] = (kinds[r.kind] || 0) + 1;

const places = [["Moiano", 214], ["Forchia", 106], ["Benevento prov.", 65], ["Arpaia", 16],
                ["Sant'Agata de' Goti", 3], ["Paolisi", 2], ["Airola", 1]];
const acts = [["births", 519], ["deaths", 147], ["marriages", 44]];
const sources = [["Antenati name index", 710],
                 ["FamilySearch — Benevento civil registration", 359],
                 ["FamilySearch, Forchia", 15], ["The family tree", 9]];
const givens = [["Maria", 45], ["Giuseppe", 41], ["Sabatino", 37], ["Antonio", 27], ["Maddalena", 26],
                ["Teresa", 23], ["Filomena", 20], ["Domenico", 19], ["Maria Rosa", 19],
                ["Mariantonia", 19], ["Pietro", 19], ["Salvatore", 18]];
---
<Base title="Annecchino"
      eyebrow="Generation seven married an Annecchino"
      dek="Now the largest surname in this archive, ahead of Falco itself — and on 10 September 2026 it gave up the name of Filomena's mother.">

  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">
    <p><strong>This page exists because its absence was noticed.</strong> The archive had been built
    outward from the Falco name, and the families that married <em>into</em> it were treated as
    context rather than as families. Annecchino is the plainest case: it had <strong>734
    records</strong> — more than any surname here except Falco — belonging to the woman who is
    <strong>generation seven's wife</strong>, and no page, no entry in the names table, and no account
    of where it comes from. Going after it properly added <strong>359 more</strong>, and made it the
    <strong>largest surname in the archive</strong>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Filomena Annecchino, and how the line runs through her</h2>
  <p class="lede"><strong>Filomena Annecchino</strong> — born <strong>10 April 1897</strong> — married
  <strong>Carminantonio Falco</strong>, born at Arienzo on 11 January 1901. She is not an in-law of
  this family. <strong>She is half of generation seven</strong>, and every Falco of the Brisbane
  generations descends from her as much as from him.</p>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>When</th><th>Where the archive gets it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Born</strong></td><td class="mono">10 Apr 1897</td>
        <td>The family tree. <strong>No birth record has been found</strong> — see the open question below.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Married <strong>Carminantonio Falco</strong></td><td class="mono">—</td>
        <td>The tree. <strong>The marriage act has not been found either.</strong> It would name both
        fathers in one line and is the single most valuable document still missing for this line.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Her son <strong>Aniello Falco</strong> dies at Forchia</td><td class="mono">2 Aug 1937</td>
        <td>Antenati name index, two entries — one of which names <em>both</em> parents,
        «Carminantonio Falco · Filomena Annecchino».</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Her daughter <strong>Maria Annunziata Falco</strong> dies at Forchia, aged about five</td>
        <td class="mono">31 Jul 1938</td>
        <td>The tree, and an Antenati death index entry of that date filed under Annecchino.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Died</strong>, Brisbane</td><td class="mono">10 Aug 1988</td>
        <td><strong>Nudgee Cemetery, niche CHAP-082-B1.</strong> Her death date was <em>blank in the
        family tree</em>; the cemetery record supplied it. She lies beside her husband in B2.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.1rem 0">
    <p><strong>Two children buried at Forchia, a year apart, before the crossing.</strong> Aniello in
    August 1937 and Maria Annunziata in July 1938. The family left for Australia after that. It is
    stated here because a line of descent that only records the survivors is not a record of a family
    — and because both deaths are indexed under <em>her</em> name, not his.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Her mother — a name at last, and what it rests on</h2>
  <p>Until 10 September 2026 this section said <em>«her mother has no name»</em>. The family tree
  carried her only as «Unknown Annecchino». A harvest of the <strong>Benevento civil registration</strong>
  through FamilySearch has now produced a candidate, and it is set out here with its reasoning so
  that a reader can weigh it rather than take it.</p>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1rem 0">
    <p style="font-size:1.05rem"><strong>Filomena Annecchino was very probably the daughter of
    CARMINE ANNECCHINO and MARIA CARFORA, of Forchia.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <h3>The household, from the indexed births</h3>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Child</th><th>Born</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Giacinta Annecchino</td><td class="mono">18 Sep 1883</td><td>Forchia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agata Annecchino</td><td class="mono">8 Oct 1884</td><td>Forchia</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Giacomo Annecchino</strong></td><td class="mono">22 Jun 1887</td><td>Forchia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agata Annecchino <span class="small">— the name given again</span></td><td class="mono">29 Jan 1889</td><td>Forchia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lucia Annecchino</td><td class="mono">2 Aug 1892</td><td>Forchia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Alfonso Annecchino <span class="small">— same day as Lucia: <strong>twins</strong></span></td><td class="mono">2 Aug 1892</td><td>Forchia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Alfonso Carmine Annecchino <span class="small">— the name given again</span></td><td class="mono">17 Jul 1894</td><td>Forchia</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="background:var(--ground)"><strong>FILOMENA — 10 Apr 1897 — the year that is missing</strong></td>
        <td class="mono">—</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Giacomo Annecchino</strong> <span class="small">— the name given again</span></td><td class="mono">2 Feb 1901</td><td>Benevento prov.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>Why it holds</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The father's name matches.</strong> The tree gives Filomena's father as
    <strong>Carmine Annecchino</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>The brother's name matches, exactly.</strong> The tree gives her brother as
    <strong>Giacomo Annecchino</strong> — and this household has <em>two</em> sons of that name, in
    1887 and again in 1901.</li>
    <li><strong>The town matches.</strong> Forchia is where Filomena's own two children died in 1937
    and 1938.</li>
    <li><strong>The date fits the gap.</strong> 10 April 1897 sits between Alfonso Carmine (July 1894)
    and the second Giacomo (February 1901) — squarely inside a childbearing span that runs 1883 to
    1901.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why it is not yet proof</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No record found names Filomena's mother.</strong> This is an argument from a household
    and a gap, not a document.</li>
    <li><strong>There is at least one other Carmine Annecchino at Forchia</strong>, married to
    <strong>Giovanna Crisci</strong>, with children indexed in 1878 and 1880. He may be a different
    man — or <em>the same man</em>, widowed and remarried about 1882, which would make Filomena's
    half-siblings older than the household above. The archive does not choose.</li>
    <li>This household lost children and reused their names <strong>three times</strong> — two Agata,
    two Alfonso, two Giacomo. Where names repeat that freely, an identification made on a forename
    needs a document behind it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why her birth act could not be found — the hole is in the harvest</h2>
  <p>The reason this took so long is worth publishing, because it tells the next reader exactly where
  to go. <strong>Filomena's birth year is missing from both indexes this archive can reach.</strong></p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>Birth years absent from the Antenati harvest, 1890–1915</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Forchia</strong></td><td class="mono">1890, <strong>1895, 1896, 1897, 1898, 1899, 1900</strong>, 1902, 1903, 1905–1911</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Arpaia</strong></td><td class="mono">1891, 1894, 1895, <strong>1897</strong>, 1903, 1905–1910</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Moiano</td><td class="mono">1895, 1898–1901, 1903–1909, 1911, 1915 — <em>1897 IS present, and holds no Filomena Annecchino</em></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>Forchia's births run 1861 to 1894 and then jump to 1901.</strong> FamilySearch's Benevento
  index has the same shape — the Carfora household's own Forchia births stop in 1894 and resume in
  1901. So the absence of a 1897 act proves nothing at all: <strong>nobody has indexed the year.</strong>
  The Forchia birth register for 1897 exists on Antenati as images. It has to be read by eye, the way
  the Arienzo death registers were.</p>

  <h2>The Annecchino of the Valle Caudina</h2>
  <p>The surname belongs to a tight cluster of towns at the head of the valley. Of the {rows.length}
  records — <strong>359 of them added on 10 September 2026</strong> from the Benevento civil
  registration — the ones that carry a place divide like this:</p>

  <div class="grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(130px,1fr));gap:.6rem;margin:.9rem 0">
    {places.map(([p, n]) => (
      <div class="card" style="padding:10px 14px"><div class="mono" style="font-size:1.4rem">{n}</div><div class="small">{p}</div></div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p><strong>Moiano, not Forchia, is the centre of gravity</strong> — 193 records against 79 — which is
  worth saying because this archive had assumed Forchia, the town the family actually married out of.
  The records run from <strong>1810 to 1942</strong>: {acts.map(([k, n], i) => (<>{i ? ", " : ""}<strong>{n}</strong> {k}</>))}.</p>

  <h2>Four Annecchino households at Forchia</h2>
  <p>These come from FamilySearch's Forchia registers and are the only Annecchino households the
  archive can currently put on the ground. <strong>None of them has been joined to Filomena</strong> —
  the link would need her birth act or her marriage act.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Father</th><th>Mother</th><th>A child, with a date</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Sabatino Annecchino</strong></td><td><strong>Mariantonia Carfora</strong></td>
        <td>Maria Filomena, 24 Dec 1864; Carmine Maria, 13 Feb 1867</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Carmine Annecchino</strong></td><td><strong>Giovanna Crisci</strong></td>
        <td>Maria Filomena, 7 Jun 1878</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Carmine Annecchino</strong></td><td><strong>Maria Carfora</strong></td>
        <td>Agata, 8 Oct 1884; Agata again, 29 Jan 1889 — <em>the name given a second time</em></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Sabatino Annecchino</strong></td><td><strong>Maria Michela Piccolo</strong></td>
        <td>Agnese, 14 Sep 1893</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small"><strong>Carfora appears twice as an Annecchino wife, and Crisci once</strong> — and
  Crisci is one of the Arienzo names that married into the Falco repeatedly. Two <em>Carmine
  Annecchino</em> with different wives are kept apart here rather than merged: this archive has been
  burned by that before, and the rule is set out under <a href={u("/method")}>Method</a>.</p>

  <h2>The commonest Annecchino forenames</h2>
  <p class="small">Useful for reading a register page: these are the names you will actually meet.</p>
  <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.4rem;margin:.7rem 0">
    {givens.map(([g, n]) => (
      <span class="chip">{g} <span class="mono">{n}</span></span>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>Who the Annecchino married</h2>
  <p>The 359 newly harvested births name both parents, which puts real couples on the ground for the
  first time. Five of the surnames they married are surnames <em>this</em> family also married:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Surname</th><th>Annecchino couples</th><th>Where else it appears here</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Carfora</strong></td><td>Carmine × <strong>Maria Carfora</strong>; Sabatino × Mariantonia Carfora</td>
        <td><strong>Filomena's probable mother</strong>, above.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Crisci</strong></td><td>Carmine × Giovanna Crisci; Antonio × Maria Giovanna Crisci; Vincenzo × Maria Crisci</td>
        <td><a href={u("/crisci")}>Generations one and three</a> of the Falco spine.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Ferraro</strong></td><td>Pietro, Domenico and Gerardo Annecchino × Maria Grazia Ferraro; Raffaele × Marianna Ferraro</td>
        <td><a href={u("/ferrara")}>Francesca Zampiello's mother</a> is written <em>Ferraro</em> in every civil record.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Maione</strong></td><td>Antonio × Anna Maria Maione; Raffaele × Anna Maria Maione</td>
        <td><a href={u("/maione")}>Generation six.</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Diglio</strong></td><td>Sabatino, Giuseppe and Gerardo Annecchino × Vittoria Diglio</td>
        <td>216 Diglio records here, all over the Arienzo registers.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small"><strong>Read this carefully rather than excitedly.</strong> These are the commonest
  surnames of a small group of neighbouring towns, and a family marrying them is a family marrying its
  neighbours. <strong>It is a pattern of regional endogamy, not evidence of a connection to the
  Falco.</strong> It is set out because it shows the world these people actually lived in — and because
  the same five names keep arriving from every direction.</p>

  <h2>What would move this line</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The FORCHIA BIRTH REGISTER FOR 1897, read by eye.</strong> This is now the single
    most valuable unread document for this family. It is on Antenati as images, it is indexed by
    nobody, and it would confirm or destroy the Maria Carfora identification in one line.</li>
    <li><strong>Her marriage to Carminantonio Falco.</strong> A Neapolitan marriage act names both
    fathers. One document would join this line to the Falco spine in print.</li>
    <li><strong>The Moiano registers.</strong> 193 Annecchino records point there and this archive has
    read none of them act by act, the way it has read Arienzo.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where these {rows.length} records come from</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Records</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{sources.map(([k, n]) => (<tr><td>{k}</td><td class="mono">{n}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small">The 359 from the Benevento civil registration were harvested on 10 September 2026
  and carry a citable collection but <strong>no per-record link</strong> — they are evidence with a
  source, not one-click documents. A further 603 rows returned by that search were
  <strong>discarded</strong>: FamilySearch's matching had fuzzed <em>Annecchino</em> into Zacchino,
  Bollecchino, Trabacchino and Lupacchino, and letting them in would have polluted the archive with
  three hundred people of another name.</p>

  <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">All {rows.length} Annecchino records are listed with their
  sources at <a href={u("/register") + "?q=Annecchino"}>the register →</a></p>
</Base>
